 ## Additional checks
 
 - Evidence cannot substitute for a verifier result.
-- Attestation binds subject, session, challenge, result reference, clock, and policy.
-- Expired and not-yet-valid appraisals deny authority.
+- Both local and peer attestation bind subject, session, challenge, result reference, clock, and policy.
+- Expired, pre-handshake and future identity/appraisal results deny on either side.
+- Pre-establishment results within the handshake are valid. now=799 succeeds before expiry 800.
 - Expired/revoked sessions, foreign clocks, and old-session requests deny authority.
 - Wrong channel, session, direction, or closed status denies authority.
-- Recovery binds the app, session, lifetime, policy, identity, and resource ceiling.
+- Recovery verification binds app/session/challenge/lifetime/identity, policy supplies the ceiling.
+- Normal and recovery ownership require a verified owner and independently resolved registry binding.
 - Normal user credentials are not required by the recovery fixture.
-- Empty recovery scope grants nothing.
+- Empty policy recovery ceiling grants nothing.
 - Policy version and digest must both match.
 - Duplicate selectors and cross-resource fallbacks are rejected.
 - A grant cannot attach to a denied outcome or contradict its request binding.
 - Primitive types are strict, including rejection of bool as an integer counter.
 - Contradictory intervals, mutable scope lists, and capability/operation mismatch fail.
 - JSON has a fixed envelope, deterministic ordering, and an exact golden vector.
-- Each authenticated metadata field changes the candidate associated-data bytes.
+- Each metadata field changes the candidate associated-data bytes. This is not replay protection.
 - HMAI has no implicit authority.
 - A denied audit record cannot claim successful execution.
 - Audit construction rejects a result from a different context snapshot.
 - Compromise and liveness remain separate policy inputs.
 
+## Cross-version regression probes
+
+All probes are in test_regressions.py and deliberately use the common fixture
+API or structural assertions. Missing 0.32 contracts cause assertion failures,
+not import errors. Each probe fails on original 0.31 and passes on 0.32.
+
+| Probe | Confirmed defect or architectural gap |
+| --- | --- |
+| r01 | Multi-field security records accepted ambiguous positional arguments |
+| r02 | READ could be relabeled as RESTRICTED while granting firmware modification |
+| r03 | Ordinary rules could grant DELETE_AUDIT_HISTORY |
+| r04 | Both identity inputs accepted a bare indefinitely reusable VERIFIED enum |
+| r05 | Audit peer_device recorded the local device |
+| r06 | Grants lacked key_epoch binding |
+| r07 | Altering ownership_binding_ref did not affect recovery authorization |
+| r08 | RecoveryVerifier returned an authority-bearing context |
+| r09 | Unenforced session.granted_scope was a misleading second authority representation |
+| r10 | Either side's attestation could predate the handshake/start boundary |
+| r11 | Ownership restoration lacked an explicit narrow access target |
+| r12 | Audit accepted different verifier/evidence provenance when the resulting authority was identical |
+
+Run `python3 tools/check_pre_fix.py --baseline ../cyberforteca-0.31`.
+Expected pre-fix result: 12 distinct failing probes, 19 assertion failures
+including subtests, zero errors. All twelve pass under ordinary 0.32 unittest.
+
+## Hardening and SMC coverage
+
+test_hardening.py parameterizes local and peer status, subject/root/enrollment,
+session, challenge, result reference, clock, policy and freshness as applicable.
+It checks the handshake bounds, grant expiry ceilings, key epoch and handle
+rotation, current-policy use-time validation, owner registry mismatches, narrow
+restoration, nominal types, audit provenance, canary references/verifier labels,
+and byte-for-byte decisions.jsonl snapshot equality.
+
+test_smc.py checks exact owner-purge authority, denial on missing rules/ceilings,
+stale grants and mismatched owners/resources, input preservation on failure,
+receipt correlation, ledger sequence/epoch validation, permanent skeleton
+preservation and deterministic hash-chain-compatible bytes. It does not test
+real file erasure, durability or cryptographic tamper resistance.
+
+## Targeted mutation check
+
+`python3 tools/check_mutations.py` copies the project into isolated temporary
+directories and removes one complete trust-check side at a time. It never edits
+the working source. A mutant only counts as killed if the appropriate status
+test fails by assertion and produces zero runtime errors.
+
+| Removed check entry | Required observation |
+| --- | --- |
+| local identity | Local UNKNOWN and REVOKED incorrectly authorize, assertions catch both |
+| peer identity | Peer UNKNOWN and REVOKED incorrectly authorize, assertions catch both |
+| local attestation | Local rejected/indeterminate/not-evaluated states incorrectly authorize, assertions catch all |
+| peer attestation | Peer rejected/indeterminate/not-evaluated states incorrectly authorize, assertions catch all |
+
+This is four targeted mutants, not a comprehensive mutation score.
+
 ## What the tests do not prove
 
-The schema's exact resource matching proves only that the simulated policy
-respects a supplied ownership ceiling. It does not prove ownership itself.
+The schema's exact resource matching checks that the simulated policy
+respects an independently supplied ownership binding. It does not prove ownership itself.
 Likewise, application matching does not prove measurement authenticity, a new
 session ID does not prove a handshake, and deterministic metadata encoding does
 not prove packet authentication. Those are explicit future adapter obligations.
